Challenges

Without bounding boxes or PNG masks, the primary challenge is guiding the model's attention to relevant parts of the image and providing it with a structured representation of the formula.

Approaches

Weakly Supervised Learning:

Attention Mechanisms: Integrate attention mechanisms into your CNN architecture. Attention mechanisms allow the model to learn to focus on important regions of the image that are most relevant to the formula's meaning. This can partially compensate for the lack of explicit bounding boxes.
Spatial Transformer Networks (STNs): STNs can be used to learn spatial transformations on the input image, allowing the model to align and normalize the formula within the image. This can improve performance when the formula's position or scale varies across images.
Data Augmentation:

Synthetic Data Generation: Consider generating synthetic data by programmatically creating images of formulas with their corresponding LaTeX labels. This can increase the size and diversity of your training data, potentially improving model generalization.
Image Transformations: Apply transformations like rotations, translations, and scaling to your existing images during training. This can help the model become more robust to variations in the formula's appearance and position within the image.
Multi-task Learning:

Auxiliary Tasks: Introduce auxiliary tasks that encourage the model to learn useful intermediate representations. For example, you could add a task to predict the type of formula (e.g., equation, integral, summation) or to identify individual symbols within the formula. This can guide the model to learn more meaningful features.
Pre-trained Models:

Transfer Learning: Utilize pre-trained CNN models (e.g., ResNet, Inception) that have been trained on large image datasets like ImageNet. Fine-tune these models on your formula dataset to leverage their learned features. This can help the model converge faster and generalize better.
Hybrid Approach:

CNN-LSTM Combination: Extract features from the raw image using a CNN and then feed those features into an LSTM to capture sequential dependencies within the formula. This can help combine the spatial awareness of CNNs with the sequential processing capabilities of LSTMs.
